WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.549
Many say size matters, but when it comes down
to business, I'm here to tell you, it's all

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:08.750
about how you position yourself.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.320
And let me tell you why this is great news
for you.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.349
Here's why.

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:14.230
Let's just say you're the David of your industry,
going against Goliath.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.210
Let's just say you're a small company, you're
a small guy.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.520
You got a smaller operation, and these people
are massive!

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.169
They're sitting on billions of dollars of
assets and you don't have that type of assets.

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:26.440
So how do you compete against them, right?

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.800
Because a lot of times I'll talk to entrepreneurs
and they'll say, "You know what, Patrick?

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.880
I have a printing company.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.720
And I want to do business cards and I also
want to do magazines and I also want to do

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.720
books.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.720
And I also want to do brochures.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.120
And I also want to do flip charts.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.440
And I also want to do this and I also want
to do banners.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.210
I want to do everything.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.719
Because I want to be able to get.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:49.390
. . there's these companies that do business
cards and they make a lot of money.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.659
What if I do all of it?"

00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:51.659
Right?

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:53.260
Or a lot of times people will say, I'm in
the financial services.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.159
I want to do real estate, I want to do loans.

00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:56.559
I want to do escrow.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:57.769
I want to do processing.

00:00:57.769 --> 00:00:58.949
I want to do underwriting.

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:00.289
I want to do title.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.999
I want to do it all!

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:05.430
Because if I do it all, I can be so big.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:11.890
That, logically, may make sense, but it's
the dumbest way for someone to position themselves.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.690
And, I'll tell you, and validate on a couple
of stories, why.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.260
I got two companies here, to give you an example
of.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.870
One, we have Heinz.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.110
The other one here we have Haloid.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.770
I'll start off with Haloid.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.490
Some of you may say, who is Haloid?

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:26.721
I don't even know who Haloid is.

00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:29.050
Who is Haloid?

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.540
Let me tell you who Haloid is.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:37.590
Haloid is actually the first company ever
to come out with personal computers.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.290
Now some of you guys are watching and saying,
c'mon, Pat.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.290
Wasn't it IBM?

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.350
IBM came out with those big computers.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.700
But Haloid is the first one that came out
with the smaller computers.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.700
The story is fascinating!

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.910
They came out with these smaller computers.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.080
They were the first ones to come out with
a mouse.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.800
Steve Jobs was the first one to launch mouse
to the world.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.280
But they were the first ones that came out
with mouse.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.410
They were the first ones that came out with
a small keyboard.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.500
It was literally this big.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.930
If you go type in right now online, Xerox
Alto.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.680
And the reason why it's called Xerox Alto
is because originally it was called Xerox

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.810
Palo Alto, they dropped the Palo.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.050
It's called Xerox Alto.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.840
It came out in 1973, stay with me here with
the numbers.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.650
When it came out in 1973, they only produced
2,000 of them.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:25.799
A thousand for their employees, 500 for universities,
and 500 they kept.

00:02:25.799 --> 00:02:28.140
They never marketed this product!

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.140
Never.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:34.900
Until one day, a guy, who lives in Palo Alto
calls them and says, hey, I hear you guys

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.319
got this machine that I want to see.

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:41.799
And I'm getting ready to start a company and
I'm willing to give you this company of mine,

00:02:41.799 --> 00:02:44.760
I'll give you 100,000 shares for a million
dollars.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.819
I just want you to allow me to see this machine.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:55.900
And this guy walks into Haloid's entire place
in 1978, 1979ish, and he walks in.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.239
And the guy's looking and says, This is a
mouse?

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:01.209
How come you're not selling this to the public?

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:02.209
Do you realize.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.450
. . and he gets antsy, and can't control himself,
jumping all over the place.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.709
They say this guy was so excited.

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:09.730
He couldn't even hold himself together.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.159
And he said, do you guys realize how powerful?

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:13.700
How many have you made of this?

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.700
Only 2,000.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.029
Oh my gosh!

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:20.030
Three days he's taking notes from morning
till night, he's just taking notes.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.030
Taking notes.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.260
He keeps coming back, keeps coming back.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.090
Taking notes, taking notes, taking notes.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.909
You know who that guy is?

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:26.909
Boom!

00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:28.129
Steve Jobs.

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:35.989
Steve Jobs goes to this place, sees a big,
dumb company, who does not realize what this

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:37.359
computer could be.

00:03:37.359 --> 00:03:42.530
The PC personal computer, if they sold it
to the public, they didn't see the vision.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.140
They fully didn't see the vision.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.969
So look what happens to them.

00:03:45.969 --> 00:03:51.079
Apple takes over, comes out and one day, Steve
Jobs launches this mouse, and everybody says,

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:53.420
"this guy's such a visionary!"

00:03:53.420 --> 00:04:00.599
He got the mouse from Haloid, Alto product,
Xerox Alto product that he saw, but he launched

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:01.599
it to the public.

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:06.760
2,000 computers, 1,000 for employees, 500
for universities, 500 they kept, Apple gave

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.799
it to the world.

00:04:07.799 --> 00:04:10.440
And Apple today is worth 400 billion dollars.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.059
Now watch what happens here.

00:04:12.059 --> 00:04:15.489
A few years later, 1973, they launch it.

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:20.919
Eight years later, Haloid decides we should
probably consider launching this computer.

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:21.919
And they do in 1980.

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:23.389
You know for how much?

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:24.949
For $100,000.

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:29.110
How many people you think can afford $100,000
computer?

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.419
In 1981!

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:33.000
Think about how much $100,000 is today.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.280
$100,000 in today's dollars is like 400, $500,000.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.990
We're talking $100,000 to buy a PC computer
in 1981.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:40.990
So guess what happens?

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.240
It flops.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.210
The best thing Haloid did, at that moment,
they realized they were way behind the eight

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.210
ball.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.949
Way behind the eight ball.

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:52.009
Apple's taking a lead on everybody.

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:56.320
Then these other companies are coming up,
the Compaq, the Atari, you know, all these

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.030
other companies are coming up.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.389
And Haloid's in the back.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:03.740
Now Haloid has a machine that they make, that's
called Xerox.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.069
And so Xerox becomes the number one copy machine.

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:09.919
On computers, Haloid is ranked #10.

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:14.180
So they said, yes, we can go compete with
computers, but you know what?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:19.220
Why don't we reposition ourselves because
it's about positioning, not size.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.990
Haloid dropped their entire computer company.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.260
They lost $84 million, when they just wrote
it off.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.580
You know what they did?

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.150
They said, "Let's just position ourselves
with copy machines."

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.879
We're not a computer company.

00:05:31.879 --> 00:05:35.240
We completely missed out here [computers],
let's go over here [copy machines].

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.900
A big, dumb company made a mistake.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.310
They were honest about the fact that they
made a mistake.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:46.080
The repositioned, and saved themselves, and
Xerox today, still, it is a Fortune 500 company,

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.280
$24.8 billion company today.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.849
They dominate the copy machine marketplace.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:54.629
They dominate the copy machine marketplace.

00:05:54.629 --> 00:05:55.629
Because they repositioned.

00:05:55.629 --> 00:05:56.629
Let me come on this side.

00:05:56.629 --> 00:06:03.139
Heinz - many, many years ago, Heinz was selling
pickles, they still do today, and they were

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:04.669
doing baby food.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:10.000
And they were so focused on baby food, and
they were so focused on pickles, but they

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.590
were always ranked top five in pickles, and
they were ranked top five in baby food.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:18.930
It was always Gerber, Vlasic, they just couldn't
beat them.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.750
When you think about pickles, you think about
Vlasic.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.230
When you think about baby food, you think
about Gerber.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:24.599
They just couldn't beat those guys.

00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:26.669
So it's like, man, let's go against size.

00:06:26.669 --> 00:06:28.949
These guys are bigger, I'm getting tired.

00:06:28.949 --> 00:06:32.200
I'm David trying to go against Goliath, who's
selling all these pickles.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.530
And they're selling all this.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:34.530
I can't.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:35.580
I'm tired.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.090
How do I go against these big guys?

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:38.259
They're killing me.

00:06:38.259 --> 00:06:39.259
Huh.

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:40.319
What are we number one in?

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:41.400
We are number one in ketchup.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.710
What the hell are we thinking about?

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.970
Why don't we just say, "Forget about this."

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.110
Let's put 5% of our time and resources into
this.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.949
Let's put 95% of our time into ketchup."

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:56.080
And so they repositioned themselves, and went
to ketchup.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.690
Today when I think about Heinz, and I tell
you Heinz, what do you think about?

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.759
Do you think about Heinz pickles?

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:01.759
C'mon.

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:02.949
You think about Heinz baby food [chuckles].

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:06.259
You don't even know Heinz has baby food, right?

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.280
You think about one thing.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.979
You think about ketchup.

00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:12.930
When I tell you Xerox today, do you even think
about Haloid?

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.810
Did you even know that Haloid started the
first personal computer?

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.009
Did you even know that Haloid came out with
the first 2,000 PCs that they only used for

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:21.189
themselves and universities.

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:22.189
Did you know that?

00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:23.189
I didn't know that.

00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:24.189
People didn't know that.

00:07:24.189 --> 00:07:25.730
Now you study and you say, Oh my.

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:26.730
They missed out.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:27.730
But they repositioned themselves.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:28.730
So now let's come back to you.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:29.730
It's all about you.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:30.979
Let's talk about you now.

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:32.969
You're running a business.

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:34.620
What are you selling?

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.470
What are you trying to sell?

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.270
Are you trying to sell anything and everything
everybody's coming up with?

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:45.219
I remember when I was running the business
and I was selling insurance, and it was securities

00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:46.219
at that time.

00:07:46.219 --> 00:07:49.159
So I'm a Series 7, 66, 31, 26 life and health.

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:50.409
I came from Morgan Stanley.

00:07:50.409 --> 00:07:51.689
So I started off with stocks.

00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:54.840
Then I transitioned to mutual funds, and money
under management.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.900
Then it was variable universal life policies
and VULs, and variable annuities.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:02.070
And then I saw everybody that was fighting
for certain pieces of the pie.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.070
I want this.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.070
I want that.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:05.070
I want this.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.219
And then finally, I had to reposition myself.

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:10.189
There was a guy in the valley, they were doing
mortgages, making $400,000 a month.

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.419
Pat, you need to get into mortgages.

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:15.580
I am this close, this close of getting into
mortgages.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:18.229
Oh, you got to get into real estate.

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:19.419
I'm this close.

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:24.169
Pat, we got to get into, I'm telling you,
title reps are making 600 grand a year.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:28.389
All you got to do is drop off some doughnuts
to these loan officers, man.

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:31.759
It's so easy, making 600 grand a year with
titles.

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:32.759
I said, "No!"

00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:33.759
Positioning.

00:08:33.759 --> 00:08:35.570
No one wants to touch life insurance.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.889
Nobody wants to touch life insurance.

00:08:37.889 --> 00:08:39.209
More business for us!

00:08:39.209 --> 00:08:40.669
No one.

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:42.640
Everybody wants to sell variable universal
life.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:43.640
Not for us.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.720
Everybody, everybody, everybody.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.070
Nope, this is our positioning.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:49.820
And we compete in this positioning.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:54.110
Today we're number one in specific products
in the entire world.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.250
We're number one in this one specific product.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:57.250
Why?

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:58.250
Positioning.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:08:59.250
Positioning.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.250
Positioning.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.790
You cannot be everything to everybody.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:02.790
Come back to you.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.630
How many different products do you sell?

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.700
Who are you trying to be?

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.170
What is your main flagship product?

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:10.360
Do you have a flagship product?

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.470
What is your flagship product?

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:13.700
What do you sell?

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:15.480
What are you mainly marketing?

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:16.860
What is it that you've got going on?

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.649
Are you positioning it right?

00:09:18.649 --> 00:09:21.750
Or are you constantly trying to fight the
big guy?

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.480
Are you trying to position yourself to say,
"I'm going to go against the big guy?"

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:26.480
Are you saying, "Nope.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.570
I'm going to reposition myself to go after
this market, because no one's touching this."

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:34.940
If you can take an hour or two, listen, New
Year's right here, it's the first video of

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.760
Valuetainment you're watching outside of probably
Motivation Monday.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.420
New Year's right here.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.860
How are you going to reposition yourself in
'17?

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:44.860
How are you going to do it?

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.480
Do you have a plan for it?

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:47.480
What are you going to be?

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.660
Do you want to be the big guy of this, that's
already there's 50 of them out there, and

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:51.660
you want to compete with this guy?

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.260
How are you going to reposition yourself?

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.870
It's almost like saying you're going to go
into politics, and you're campaigning, and

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.310
everybody's campaigning around let's pick
something, give me a subject on politics that

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.329
some people campaign around.

00:10:04.329 --> 00:10:05.329
Let's just say economics.

00:10:05.329 --> 00:10:07.320
Let's just say pro-life, pro-choice.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.639
Let's just say you got military.

00:10:08.639 --> 00:10:10.600
Let's just say you got this.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.730
And it's like you're running for campaign,
and you're saying, I'm going to build a wall.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:15.470
You know why you say that?

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.470
It's positioning.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.120
No one's talking about that.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.870
And then all the politicians are saying, what?

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:23.190
The same old thing.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:25.450
And they lose.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:26.970
And they lose.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.529
Some people look at Valuetainment and say,
Pat, how come it's not all the motivation

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:32.300
like some of these other channels all the
time.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:33.730
You're actually doing how-to.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:36.750
Man, we repositioned ourselves.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:37.750
We get a lot of heat.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:41.980
We get a lot of mail and hate mail, Pat, we
think what you're doing is wrong.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:43.040
What am I doing wrong?

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.930
You shouldn't give all this content out for
free.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.850
You should sell it for a few thousand dollars.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:48.699
I'm not doing that.

